      "card_ids": ["C12"],
      "claim": "Loxapine, the closest analogue (Morgan 0.451), has a human systemic availability of 29%, which is above the 20% threshold despite substantial first-pass extraction; this supports high bioavailability for the query because the query shares the chlorinated dibenzoxazepine-like core and N-methylamine.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C27"],
      "claim": "Clomipramine, a tricyclic antidepressant with a chloro substituent and a tertiary amine, has an absolute oral bioavailability of about 48% in healthy volunteers, supporting high bioavailability for the query.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C58"],
      "claim": "Mirtazapine, a tetracyclic antidepressant with an N-methylpiperazine-like motif, has an absolute oral bioavailability of about 50% in humans, supporting high bioavailability for the query.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C32"],
      "claim": "Prochlorperazine, a tricyclic phenothiazine with a chloro substituent and N-methylpiperazine side chain, has a low absolute oral bioavailability of about 12.5%, which is contradictory evidence; however, its lower similarity and different heterocyclic core reduce transferability to the query.",
      "evidence_role": "contradictory"
    }
  ],
  "confidence": "moderate",
  "decision_summary": "The closest analogue loxapine has F=29%, above the 20% threshold, and several structurally related tricyclic/tetracyclic amines have F≈50-99%. The only clearly low-F analogue, prochlorperazine, is less similar. Overall evidence supports F≥20%.",
